  "title": "Whose, possessive ‘s: Whose is this? It’s Mike’s",
  "source_url": "https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/whose-possessive-s/",
  "scraped_at": "2026-05-23T07:02:33.220Z",
  "explanation": {
    "markdown": "## Whose, possessive ‘s: Whose is this? It’s Mike’s\n\n### Possessive **‘s**: Use and form\n\n![Informative grammar chart explaining the use and form of possessive 's in English, with rules for singular, plural, and irregular nouns, and examples.](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-1.png)\n\n### Possessive **‘s**: Use\n\nWe use **‘s** to show that something belongs to a person (or a pet) or to talk about relationships between people.\n\n-   *This is Peter**‘s father**. ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-2.png)* \n-   *This is **the father of** Peter.![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-3.png)*\n-   *Peter and Mary**‘s** car is red.* \n-   *My cat**‘s** ears are white.* \n\nWe also use the possessive **‘s** to talk about shops and houses.\n\n-   *I am at John**‘s**.* (= at John’s house)\n-   *I need to go to the chemist**‘s**.* (= the chemist’s shop)\n\n### Possessive **‘s**: Form\n\nSingular noun + **‘s**\n\n-   ***Anna’s** clothes, **Chris’s** wife, the **student’s** books, etc.*\n\nPlural noun ending in **\\-s** + **‘** (apostrophe)\n\n-   *The **students’** books, the **boys’** toys, the **teachers’** lounge, etc.*\n\nIrregular plural noun (NOT ending in **\\-s**) + **‘s**\n\n-   *The **children’s** toys, **men’s** clothes, etc.*\n\nWhen one thing belongs to two or more people, add **‘s** only after the last noun.\n\n-   ***Paul and Katherine’s** house, **Anna and George’s** car, etc.*\n\nWhen we have two or more people, and each person has one thing, we add **‘s** after each person.\n\n-   ***Anna’s and George’s** cars* (Anna’s car and George’s car), ***Sally’s** and **Tim’s** computers* (Sally’s computer and Tim’s computer).\n\n### Possessive **of**\n\nWe use **of** and NOT **‘s** when we talk about things (and not people or pets).\n\n-   *the end **of** the street ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-4.png)* \n-   *the street**‘s** end ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-5.png)* \n-   *a picture **of** the eclipse ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-6.png)* \n-   *the eclipse**‘s** picture ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-7.png)* \n-   *the man **of** the match ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-8.png)* \n-   *the match**‘s** man ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-9.png)* \n\n### Compound nouns: the city centre\n\nWith nouns that are used together very often, we often use them together without **of** and without **‘s**\n\n-   *a **school bus***\n-   *the **city centre***\n-   *the **car key***\n\n### **Whose**\n\n#### When do we use **whose**?\n\nWe use the question word **whose** to ask about possession. There are two possible forms:\n\n**whose** + noun\n\n-   ***Whose** car is this?*\n-   ***Whose** books are those?* \n\n**whose** without a noun\n\n-   ***Whose** is this car?*\n-   ***Whose** are those books?* \n\nWhen we answer with the possessive **‘s**, we can also use **‘s** \\+ noun or **‘s** without a noun.\n\n-   ***Whose** is this car?*\n-   *It’s John**‘s car***\n-   *It’s John**‘s**.* \n\n#### **Whose** vs **who’s**\n\n**Whose** = possession (of who)\n\n**Who’s** = who is\n\n-   ***Whose** car is this?* *![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-10.png)*\n-   ***Who’s** car is this?* *![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-11.png)*\n-   ***Who’s** that man in the lobby? ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-12.png)* \n-   ***Whose** that man in the lobby? ![](/uploads/grammar/a1/whose-possessive-s/img-13.png)*",
